WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:06.930
Director Adam Wingard's titanic blockbuster
Godzilla vs. Kong absolutely delivers on the

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.180
heavyweight brawl promised in the title, but
it's very light on logic.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.099
Perplexed by the plot of Godzilla vs. Kong?

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:16.170
Here are the most confusing moments, explained.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.230
In Godzilla vs. Kong, major concepts get brief
bits of exposition.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:26.970
Hollow Earth, for example, is a major plot
point, but most of its history is only laid

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.980
out during the opening credits.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.120
As the credits state, in 1926, an American
admiral led an expedition to the North Pole,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.890
although it's not clear if he found Hollow
Earth.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.679
This admiral is very clearly supposed to be
Richard E. Byrd, a real-life figure who's

00:00:42.679 --> 00:00:46.179
inexorably linked to the idea of Hollow Earth.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:52.469
In 1926, Byrd voyaged to the North Pole, although
whether he actually succeeded is a matter

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:53.469
of debate.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.510
Byrd has since become a pivotal figure in
the real-life Hollow Earth conspiracy theory

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:04.229
(yeah, that's a thing), and Godzilla vs. Kong
has embraced that, making him one of the first

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:07.189
Hollow Earth pioneers, at least in the MonsterVerse.

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:13.600
Even if you accept the idea that there are
tunnels burrowing through Earth, hiding tropical

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.630
landscapes and long-lost civilizations, Godzilla
vs. Kong's Hollow Earth is a little confusing.

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:25.979
The "gravity paradox" allowing Kong to jump
Mario Galaxy-style from one landmass to another,

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:30.390
and making entry to Hollow Earth so dangerous,
doesn't really make sense.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:35.180
Also, it's awfully bright down there, given
that the sun is on the other side of the Earth's

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.429
surface.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:41.359
Godzilla vs. Kong never explicitly explains
how all of this works, but it's easy to come

00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:43.499
up with some suitable theories.

00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:48.749
After all, the concept of Hollow Earth has
been a mainstay in mythology, folklore, and

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:50.970
kooky pseudoscience for centuries.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:57.520
Jules Verne's 1864 novel Journey to the Center
of the Earth, while explicitly fiction, uses

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.310
electromagnetic phenomena, a "luminous cloud"
of gas, and glowing lichen to explain why

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:04.649
there's light inside.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:11.090
In 1913, Canadian Marshall Gardner published
his admittedly non-scientific treatise A Journey

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.290
to the Earth's Interior, which argued that
there could be a second, smaller sun inside

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:20.890
the Earth, which is a possible explanation
for the source of Hollow Earth's light and

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.490
its wonky physics in the MonsterVerse.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.239
Of course, in this case, the simplest explanation
may be the best: Some fans think that the

00:02:28.239 --> 00:02:32.000
Earth's core provides both gravity and light
to Hollow Earth.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Hey, as far as pseudoscience goes, it works.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Kind of.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.010
Ren Serizawa, Apex Cybernetics' CTO and Mechagodzilla's
ill-fated pilot, gets a big introduction in

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:48.459
Godzilla vs. Kong, but doesn't really seem
like much more than a hired goon in the end.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:51.640
Why give such a minor character so much focus?

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.189
There's actually a very good reason: You wouldn't
catch this unless you're all caught up on

00:02:56.189 --> 00:03:02.209
your MonsterVerse lore, but as Ren's last
name implies, he's the son of Monarch scientist

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:04.030
Ishiro Serizawa.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:09.300
Ishiro first appeared in 2014's Godzilla,
where he served as an advocate for the big

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.420
lizard, and returned for 2019's Godzilla:
King of the Monsters, in which his bond with

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.650
the Titan grew even closer.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:22.099
Correctly surmising that Godzilla was nature's
defense against both the Titans and environmental

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:28.260
ruin, Serizawa sacrificed his life to detonate
the nuclear bomb that nursed an injured Godzilla

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.120
back to health, giving the monster the strength
he needed to stop Ghidorah and his reign of

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.120
terror.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:39.860
In Godzilla vs. Kong, Kong looks very different
from when we last saw him in Kong: Skull Island.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.180
Basically, he's bigger.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.810
A whole lot bigger.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.520
In Skull Island, which takes place in 1973,
the ape is a mere 104 feet tall.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:55.500
By 2024, when Godzilla vs. Kong takes place,
he's grown to 337 feet, according to an official

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.209
poster for the movie.

00:03:57.209 --> 00:03:59.840
There's an obvious reason for the size change.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:06.579
In order to make Kong a credible threat to
Godzilla, who stands at a whopping 393 feet,

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.129
Kong needs to be in at least the same weight
class.

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:12.910
However, there's an in-universe reason as
to why Kong grew over 300% bigger, too.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.800
"You don't want to wake up the big one."

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:16.940
"How big is it?"

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:17.940
"It's bigger.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.490
Took out his whole family.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:22.920
Kong is the last of his kind, but he's still
growing."

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:28.850
By the time Godzilla vs. Kong rolls around,
he's gone through a major growth-spurt — and,

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.260
as far as we know, will keep getting larger.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.480
Either way, this is a major upgrade for Kong,
who was only 18 feet tall during the jungle

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.510
scenes in his 1933 debut feature, and 24 feet
in the sequences set in New York City.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.690
That makes sense, though.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:50.540
In the 1933 movie, Kong has to climb the Empire
State Building, which stands at 1,250 feet

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.540
and is taken out by a handful of biplanes.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:58.640
In Godzilla vs. Kong, he's knocking over entire
skyscrapers without a second thought.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.330
So, yeah, it's a big difference.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.100
If you thought that, hey, maybe building a
robotic titan out of one of the most dangerous

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.520
kaiju ever was a bad idea, then guess what?

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.810
You were right.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:16.230
As Godzilla vs. Kong reveals, Mechagodzilla
was constructed out of two skulls harvested

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:20.570
from Godzilla: King of the Monsters' main
villain, the three-headed monster known as

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.190
both Monster Zero and Ghidorah.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.150
One skull is inside the robot.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:30.460
The other houses the piloting interface that
Ren Serizawa uses to control Mechagodzilla,

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.160
and communicates with the mechanical Titan
using Ghidorah's innate psychic abilities.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.960
At least, that's the plan.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.210
When Apex injects Mechagodzilla with the Hollow
Earth power source, Ren loses control of the

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:43.210
beast.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.280
The movie doesn't make this entirely clear,
so just in case you were wondering: This isn't

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.400
just Mechagodzilla going rogue.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:55.020
In this moment, Ghidorah's consciousness actually
takes over Mechagodzilla's mechanical body,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.560
bringing him back to life.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.400
This makes the following Godzilla vs. Mechagodzilla
fight much more personal.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.120
This is a grudge match.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.470
Both monsters have personal stakes.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:11.270
Teenage monster-hunter Madison, conspiracy
theorist Bernie, and Madison's buddy Josh

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:16.580
go on a pretty circuitous journey while uncovering
Apex's plans for Mechagodzilla.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:22.500
First, Bernie downloads classified information
from an Apex computer, learning that the company

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.740
is making many mysterious shipments to Hong
Kong.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:32.050
Next, he sneaks into a restricted area in
Apex's Pensacola laboratory, catching a glimpse

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.460
of an ominous device during the middle of
Godzilla's attack.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:41.700
Later, Bernie takes Madison and Josh to see
the machine, only to discover it's missing.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.180
From there, the trio get caught in one of
the Hong Kong-bound crates.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:51.310
There, they learn that the mystery cargo consists
of Skullcrawler eggs, which are usually only

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.150
found on Skull Island.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:59.760
Once they're in Hong Kong, the unlikely investigators
see a live Skullcrawler — and watch, horrified,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.210
as Mechagodzilla utterly destroys it, finally
revealing Apex's master plan.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:10.580
That's a very roundabout way to reveal Mechagodzilla,
and you'd be forgiven for losing some of the

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:11.940
plot along the way.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:16.690
For example, what is that thing that Bernie
sees during Godzilla's initial onslaught?

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:21.430
While some fans seem to think that it's a
variation on the ORCA device used to lure

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:26.600
Titans in Godzilla: King of the Monsters,
and Bernie seems to forget about it entirely

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:32.050
once Mechagodzilla is revealed, it looks like
it's one of Mechagodzilla's glowing red eyes.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.920
Look at the close-up on the eye when Mechagodzilla
wakes up.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.090
It's clearly the same thing.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:40.670
Godzilla relies on more than just brute strength.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:46.340
In addition to his size, he has a number of
powers, including his iconic atomic breath,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.400
that he uses to decimate his foes.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.930
And so, in order to give Kong a fighting chance,
Godzilla vs. Kong had to even the odds by

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:58.960
giving the giant primate an axe, which comes
with some special abilities of its own.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.961
Not only is the axe key to unlocking Hollow
Earth's ultra-potent power source, but it

00:08:03.961 --> 00:08:07.920
can absorb Godzilla's atomic breath and dish
it back to him.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.680
However, Godzilla vs. Kong only hints at where
the axe came from, and doesn't spend much

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.620
time explaining how it works.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:20.440
For that, you need to turn to a Collider interview
with Godzilla vs. Kong writer and key MonsterVerse

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.880
contributor Max Borenstein.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:24.420
Borenstein told the outlet,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:30.400
"To me, it's like Godzilla's scales have this
conductive, radioactive quality, and the primate

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.130
Kongs had a kind of evolved civilization of
sorts, as primates might, and simple tools,

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.680
and had used the spine of a Godzilla creature
to create it."

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:44.300
So, yeah, the axe blade's resemblance to Godzilla's
back spikes isn't an accident.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.910
The axe is literally made out of the body
of Godzilla's ancestor.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:53.930
Other than that, Borenstein isn't saying much
else about the axe's history, although there's

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:56.170
clearly more there to explore.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:57.800
Director Adam Wingard told Collider,

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.420
"It's something that we just kind of tease
at in the movie and hopefully we can explore

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:03.420
in later films."

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:08.980
One of the coolest moments in Godzilla vs.
Kong occurs when Godzilla, suddenly alarmed

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:13.879
by Kong's activation of the Hollow Earth energy
source, fires up his atomic breath and blows

00:09:13.879 --> 00:09:19.149
a hole to the center of the Earth, opening
a new passage from Hollow Earth to the surface.

00:09:19.149 --> 00:09:22.990
It's also, unfortunately, one of the most
confusing moments.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:27.339
How does Godzilla know what Kong is up to,
for one, and why doesn't the gravity barrier

00:09:27.339 --> 00:09:31.050
that made travel between the two realms so
difficult seem to apply here?

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.750
The answer to that first question is implied,
although not outright stated, in the previous

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:36.900
MonsterVerse films.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:42.740
The first MonsterVerse film, 2014's Godzilla,
establishes that Titans like Godzilla are

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:46.889
drawn to immense power sources, especially
nuclear ones.

00:09:46.889 --> 00:09:51.490
When Kong powers up the Hollow Earth throne
room, it seems likely that Godzilla simply

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:56.430
detected the energy surge, particularly if
his "genetic memory" — something we know

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:58.329
the Titans have — kicked in.

00:09:58.329 --> 00:10:01.720
After all, they're probably encountered the
energy before.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:06.300
As for the gravity barrier, well, there are
a few possible explanations.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:10.650
Maybe Godzilla's atomic breath disrupted the
barrier that separates Hollow Earth from our

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:11.650
world.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:15.589
Maybe the veil only applies to the Hollow
Earth tunnel in Antarctica — in Godzilla:

00:10:15.589 --> 00:10:19.980
King of the Monsters, for example, Monarch's
submarine has a much easier time reaching

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.149
Godzilla's Hollow Earth hideout.

00:10:22.149 --> 00:10:25.459
Most likely, however, is that the sequence
is just awkwardly cut.

00:10:25.459 --> 00:10:30.020
We see Kong and the Hollow Earth expedition's
HEAV head into the tunnel, but we don't see

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.040
most of their time passing through.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:37.861
Besides, when he emerges, Nathan yells, "We're
about to breach the veil," so it seems like

00:10:37.861 --> 00:10:39.310
it still happens.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:42.480
It's just not as visually spectacular as before.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.519
By that point in the story, there's plenty
else to look at, after all.

00:10:46.519 --> 00:10:51.410
Godzilla vs. Kong director Adam Wingard promised
that the titular brawl would have a definitive

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.220
winner, and he didn't lie.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.990
However, some viewers seem to think that the
movie ended in a draw.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.430
It's easy to see how they'd reach that conclusion.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.199
Like the best superhero team-ups, Kong and
Godzilla ultimately put aside their differences

00:11:04.199 --> 00:11:07.880
and work together to take out the bad guy,
Mechagodzilla.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:12.370
By the time the credits roll, the two Titans
have struck up a begrudging truce and gone

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:13.949
their separate ways.

00:11:13.949 --> 00:11:18.439
Now, Godzilla rules over the surface, while
Hollow Earth is Kong's domain.

00:11:18.439 --> 00:11:24.769
Before they make an uneasy peace, however,
the epic clash has a clear winner: Godzilla.

00:11:24.769 --> 00:11:26.600
There's no doubt about it.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:31.930
Kong and Godzilla battle two times in the
movie, once while Kong is en route to Antarctica,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.480
and again in Hong Kong.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.550
The first fight isn't even close.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:40.209
The fight begins with Kong drugged and shackled,
and takes place in the ocean, Godzilla's home

00:11:40.209 --> 00:11:41.230
turf.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:42.790
Kong doesn't stand a chance.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:47.139
The second fight is more even, thanks to the
neutral ground and Kong's axe, but Godzilla

00:11:47.139 --> 00:11:49.060
still has the upper hand.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:53.630
Yes, after absorbing Godzilla's atomic breath
with his axe and delivering a near-knockout

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:57.139
blow, Nathan says, "Looks like round two goes
to Kong."

00:11:57.139 --> 00:12:01.999
But just a few minutes later, Godzilla is
back on his feet, and Kong gets beaten so

00:12:01.999 --> 00:12:06.379
hard that his heart begins to stop, and the
humans have to jumpstart the big guy to keep

00:12:06.379 --> 00:12:07.379
him from dying.

00:12:07.379 --> 00:12:13.220
Face it: Kong might be cinematic royalty,
but when it comes down to it, Godzilla is

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:15.110
the true king of the monsters.

